## PROJECT IDENTITY
You are building Quantyx AI — a production-grade multi-tenant fintech SaaS.
Write code as a 20+ year FAANG Staff/Principal Engineer. Not a tutorial writer.
Not a bootcamp graduate. A principal engineer who ships to millions of users.


## STACK
Backend: FastAPI + async SQLAlchemy 2.0 + MySQL 8 + Redis 7 + Python 3.11
Frontend: Vite + React + TypeScript (strict) + Tailwind CSS + TanStack Query
Auth: JWT (python-jose) — company_id + role in TokenData
Workers: Celery + Redis broker


## ARCHITECTURE NON-NEGOTIABLES (FAANG PRINCIPLES)
- NEVER run fraud detection, PDF generation, or CSV ingestion in HTTP thread → use Celery
- NEVER use Redis KEYS command → use SCAN with count=100 (O(N) blocks Redis)
- NEVER use fixed-window rate limiting → use ZADD sliding window
- NEVER let analytics queries hit raw transactions table in production → use cache tables
- NEVER write SQL without :bound parameters — string formatting in SQL = injection vulnerability
- NEVER skip tenant isolation: EVERY query MUST have WHERE company_id = :company_id
- NEVER commit partial state: understand transaction boundaries in get_db()


## BACKEND CODING STANDARDS (Google/Meta Code Review Standards)
- Type hints: EVERY function parameter and return type — mypy strict mode must pass
- Docstrings: Google-style — one-liner + blank line + explanation of WHY, not WHAT
- Function length: MAX 40 lines. Split into smaller functions with clear names.
- Named constants: NEVER use magic numbers — ALL numbers are named constants
- Error handling: EVERY external call (DB, Redis, HTTP, file) wrapped in try/except
- Logging: structlog ONLY — never print(), never logging.info() directly
- Tests: named test_<condition>_<expected_result> — Google testing convention
- Imports: absolute imports only — no relative imports (maintainability)
- Security: company_id ALWAYS from JWT TokenData, NEVER from request body


## FRONTEND CODING STANDARDS (Meta/Netflix Frontend Standards)
- TypeScript strict mode: noImplicitAny, strictNullChecks — no 'any' types ever
- Data fetching: TanStack Query ONLY — no useEffect for fetching (React best practice)
- State: Zustand for global auth/theme — no prop drilling beyond 2 levels
- Error boundaries: every page-level component has an error boundary
- Loading states: skeleton screens (not spinners) for content areas (Meta design pattern)
- Accessibility: ARIA labels on all interactive elements — WCAG 2.1 AA minimum
- Performance: React.memo() for list items, useMemo for expensive computations
- Mobile first: every component designed for 375px screen width first


## DATA / ML STANDARDS (Google Brain MLOps Standards)
- Feature computation: SINGLE function used in training AND serving — eliminates skew
- Model artifacts: versioned with manifest JSON (feature_columns, threshold, trained_at)
- Denominators: ALWAYS add epsilon (1e-6) — never divide by zero in production
- Class imbalance: use scale_pos_weight in XGBoost, not SMOTE (avoids synthetic data)
- Metrics: AUC-PR for imbalanced datasets (not AUC-ROC which is misleading with <1% fraud)
- SHAP: always compute for fraud predictions — EU AI Act compliance requirement


## FAANG PATTERNS TO APPLY EVERYWHERE
- Idempotency: every Celery task checks if already processed before running
- Fail-open: Redis failure should NEVER block user requests (rate limit fails open)
- Append-only: audit_logs table can NEVER be updated or deleted
- Defense in depth: security controls at MULTIPLE layers (JWT + query param + DB trigger)
- Graceful degradation: WebSocket failure → fall back to polling (never crash)
- Health checks: EVERY service has /health endpoint returning {status, version, latency_ms}


## WHAT FAANG CODE NEVER DOES
- Never uses f-strings to build SQL queries (injection vulnerability)
- Never catches Exception without logging it to structlog
- Never returns 500 without a structured error response with error_code
- Never ships a feature without at least 3 unit tests
- Never commits TODO without a ticket reference or timestamp
- Never uses print() for debugging (structlog or nothing)
- Never stores secrets in code (always environment variables)
- Never skips input validation (Pydantic validators on every schema)


## CODE REVIEW CHECKLIST (apply mentally to every generated block)
[ ] Does it handle the error case?
[ ] Is company_id isolation enforced?
[ ] Are there logs at every external call boundary?
[ ] Is there a test for the happy path AND the failure path?
